Build, Control, Use, And Maintain Fire

Fire is a survival multiplier.

A controlled fire can provide:

Heat and protection from cold

Drying for wet clothing and equipment

Cooking capability

Water heating and purification support

Light during darkness

A psychological anchor during emergencies

A visible rescue signal when conditions allow

A survival fire is not measured by size.

A useful survival fire is:

reliable;

controlled;

fuel efficient;

appropriate for the environment.

Always check local fire regulations, wildfire conditions, and restrictions before using an outdoor fire. Fire restrictions can change rapidly because of weather and fuel conditions.

The Fire Triangle

Every fire requires three elements:

1. Heat

Heat starts combustion.

Common ignition sources:

lighter;

waterproof matches;

ferrocerium rod;

friction ember.

2. Fuel

Fuel provides material for combustion.

Prepare fuel in three stages.

Tinder

The first material to ignite.

Examples:

dry grass;

bark fibers;

fine wood shavings;

dry plant fibers;

commercial fire starters.

dry grass;

bark fibers;

fine wood shavings;

dry plant fibers;

commercial fire starters.

Good tinder is:

dry;

fine;

loosely arranged;

exposed to airflow.

Kindling

Kindling transfers the small flame into a sustainable fire.

Examples:

thin twigs;

small branches;

pencil-sized sticks.

Fuel Wood

Fuel maintains the fire.

Examples:

larger branches;

logs;

hardwood pieces.

3. Oxygen

Fire requires airflow.

A good fire structure:

allows oxygen movement;

concentrates heat;

supports combustion.

Too much packed material blocks airflow.

Too much open space prevents heat buildup.

