Overview

Understand mushroom risk.

Wild mushrooms can provide food, but incorrect identification can cause severe poisoning.

Identification Requirements

Reliable identification requires:

species knowledge;

habitat knowledge;

season awareness;

expert confirmation.

Do Not Rely On:

color alone;

smell;

animal feeding behavior;

folklore tests.

Overview

Understand when plants can provide food resources.

Wild plants may provide:

carbohydrates;

vitamins;

minerals;

emergency calories.

The Identification Rule

Only consume wild plants that are positively identified.

Correct identification requires knowing:

species;

edible parts;

preparation requirements;

local distribution.

Poisonous plants can resemble edible species, and safe foraging requires proper knowledge and identification.

Step 1

Observe the complete plant.

Check:

leaves;

stem;

flowers;

roots;

seeds;

growth pattern.

Step 2

Compare Multiple Features

Never rely on a single characteristic.

Step 3

Confirm Identification

Use:

regional field guides;

expert knowledge;

previous training.

Edible Plant Parts

Depending on species:

Leaves

May provide nutrients.

Roots

May provide stored carbohydrates.

Seeds

May provide fats and proteins.

Fruits

May provide sugars and vitamins.

Know Where You Are, Where You Are Going, And How To Be Found

Navigation is the skill of controlling movement through the environment.

A survival navigator must understand:

current location;

direction of travel;

terrain around them;

route options;

emergency communication.

The goal of navigation is not only reaching a destination.

The goal is:

KNOW YOUR POSITION

-

MOVE SAFELY

-

MAKE GOOD DECISIONS

-

BE FOUND IF SOMETHING GOES WRONG

The Navigation Survival Principle

Reliable navigation uses multiple systems together:

1. Map

Provides:

terrain information;

elevation;

water features;

trails;

roads;

landmarks.

2. Compass

Provides:

direction;

bearings;

orientation.

3. Terrain Awareness

Uses:

ridges;

valleys;

slopes;

rivers;

landmarks.

4. Technology

Includes:

GPS;

offline mapping;

satellite messenger;

emergency beacon.

Modern navigation works best as a layered system. A map and compass provide a backup when electronics fail, while GPS and communication devices improve location accuracy and emergency response capability.

