Protect Your Body From The Environment

Shelter is the skill of controlling your exposure to the environment.

A person can become exhausted or injured from:

cold;

heat;

wind;

rain;

snow;

sun exposure.

A survival shelter does not need to be large.

A good shelter is:

fast to build;

placed in a safe location;

insulated;

suitable for the environment.

The purpose of shelter is:

maintain body temperature;

reduce exposure;

conserve energy;

create a safe resting area.

1. Location

The best shelter starts with the correct location.

A poor location can make a good shelter unsafe.

2. Insulation

Reduce heat loss by trapping air and separating the body from cold surfaces.

3. Weather Protection

Protect against:

wind;

rain;

snow;

extreme sunlight.

4. Energy Efficiency

The shelter should save energy, not consume all available energy to build.

Survival Goal

Protect against:

direct sunlight;

heat gain;

dehydration.

Location

Choose:

natural shade;

rocky protection;

areas away from flood channels.

Design Principles

A desert shelter should:

block sunlight;

allow airflow;

create shade;

reduce exposure.

